# THE FLEET MEMORY BUDGET: one number for the whole box.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Docker `mem_limit` bounds ONE container, and it did its
# job (2026-08-05: no container was OOM-killed). It says nothing about the SUM.
# 22 chains, each legitimately under its own ceiling, still exhausted a 61 GiB
# box collectively, and a kernel in global direct reclaim cannot schedule sshd,
# which is how the box became unreachable with TCP/22 open and no banner. The
# per-container ceilings are DELIBERATELY oversubscribed (~130 GB of ceilings on
# 61 GiB) because a ceiling is not a reservation and a chain that needs to burst
# should be able to; the missing instrument was a bound on the total.
#
# WHAT IT DOES. Every epochdb container runs inside this slice, so the kernel
# accounts them as one. MemoryHigh is the fleet's working-set target: past it
# the kernel reclaims the SLICE's own pages (the chunk-cache page cache and the
# epoch mappings first, since they are clean file pages) and throttles the
# slice's allocations. That is the required failure mode, DEGRADE BY SLOWING
# DOWN: chains get slower and their reads get colder, and nothing dies.
# MemoryMax is the backstop, and the important part is WHOSE backstop: hitting
# it OOM-kills one container inside the slice (restart: unless-stopped brings it
# back) instead of putting the whole box into global reclaim. Everything outside
# this slice, sshd included, is never squeezed by the fleet at all.
#
# THE ARITHMETIC on the current box (i7i.2xlarge, 61.78 GiB usable, no swap):
#
# MemoryHigh MUST STAY ABOVE THE SUM OF THE MEMBERS' ANON WORKING SETS, not just
# above "most of the box". 2026-08-07, one giant chain (mainnet C) plus one small
# one sat at 47.88 GB against a 47.24 GB MemoryHigh, i.e. PERMANENTLY over it, so
# the kernel reclaimed the slice continuously: memory PSI full avg10 64.85%,
# mainnet at 83.7 mgas/s. Raising MemoryHigh to 49G stopped it dead (the `high`
# counter in memory.events froze) and PSI fell to 0.4%. The throttle band is a
# band to pass THROUGH under burst, not a line to live on: when the steady state
# is above MemoryHigh there is no "degrade by slowing down", only degrade.
#
# THE MEMBER CEILING MUST ALSO LEAVE ROOM FOR PAGE CACHE, and this is the part
# that is easy to get wrong because `docker stats` does not separate it. Read
# memory.stat: mainnet's ANON alone was 43.97 GB (40.9 GiB) against a 44 GiB
# mem_limit, so ~2 GiB was left for file pages and every Firewood read went to
# disk. Raising that one container to 48g gave it ~7.3 GB of page cache and
# measured +30% throughput (27 -> 35 blk/s, 83.7 -> 133.5 mgas/s), no code change
# and no restart (`docker update --memory`, which GOMEMLIMIT ignores because the
# process read its ceiling at startup). Size a member as anon + its cache, never
# anon alone.
#
#   MemoryHigh=49G   the fleet's target. Sized against the measured anon terms:
#                    per chain, Firewood's cgo node cache (3% of its Firewood
#                    dir, 64MB..4GB), the exec state cache (an eighth of the
#                    container's ceiling since 2026-08-05), the per-block index
#                    every open store pays over its unsealed tail (~182 B/block,
#                    which is ~1.2 GB for a sparse L1 at the 8M-tx epoch
#                    ceiling), and the epoch blooms, which are on the Go heap.
#                    At 22 chains that totals ~30-35 GiB, and the rest of the
#                    49G is the fleet's page cache.
#   MemoryMax=52G    hard backstop: ~9.8 GiB of the box is never docker's.
#   CPUWeight=50     the other half of "sshd always runs". Weights only bind
#                    under contention (the box was at load 8.14 on 8 vCPU when
#                    it stopped answering), and system.slice is idle almost
#                    always, so this costs the fleet nothing in practice.
#
# RE-DERIVE BEFORE CHANGING THE BOX SIZE: high ~= 70% of RAM, max ~= 85%. Both
# are box facts, not chain facts, so they do not change when chains are added.
# Adding chains changes what happens INSIDE the budget, which is the point.
#
# CHECK FIRST, one command: `docker info --format '{{.CgroupDriver}}'` must say
# `systemd` (the default on cgroup v2 hosts, which AL2023 is). On the cgroupfs
# driver docker makes a literal directory of that name instead of asking systemd
# for the slice, and these limits would not be applied to it; the fix is
# "exec-opts": ["native.cgroupdriver=systemd"] in /etc/docker/daemon.json plus a
# daemon restart.
#
# APPLY (nothing here is applied to a running box by anything but you). The unit
# must exist BEFORE a container names the slice, or systemd creates a transient
# one with no limits at all:
#
#   sudo install -m 0644 epochdb.slice /etc/systemd/system/epochdb.slice
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload
#
# Then put the containers in it, and do it PER SERVICE, in the compose file,
# because that is the staggered path: recreating one chain at a time is the
# normal procedure on this box, while the fleet-wide alternative below restarts
# the docker daemon and therefore all 22 chains at once, which is the stampede
# this budget exists to survive rather than to cause. In each service block:
#
#   services:
#     mainnet-c:
#       cgroup_parent: epochdb.slice     # <- the only new line
#       mem_limit: 24g                   # unchanged: the member's own ceiling
#       ...
#
#   cd /data/epochdb && docker compose up -d mainnet-c   # one chain, then next
#   systemd-cgls /epochdb.slice | head                   # it really landed there
#
# FLEET-WIDE ALTERNATIVE, right on the next box build or after a reboot, where a
# daemon restart is free and no future container can forget the line:
# `{ "cgroup-parent": "epochdb.slice" }` in /etc/docker/daemon.json, then
# `sudo systemctl restart docker`.
#
# WATCH IT WORK (see also DESIGN.md "The fleet memory budget"):
#
#   cat /sys/fs/cgroup/epochdb.slice/memory.current   # the fleet's total
#   cat /sys/fs/cgroup/epochdb.slice/memory.events    # high=N is the budget
#                                                     # holding; oom_kill>0 is
#                                                     # the budget too tight
#   cat /sys/fs/cgroup/epochdb.slice/memory.pressure  # some avg60 climbing is
#                                                     # the throttle band
#   free -g                                           # `available` must never
#                                                     # approach zero again

[Unit]
Description=epochdb chain fleet (one process per chain, one budget for the box)
Before=docker.service

[Slice]
MemoryAccounting=yes
MemoryHigh=49G
MemoryMax=52G
CPUAccounting=yes
CPUWeight=50
